<main class="wrap">
 <a class="back" href="/projects">← All projects</a>
 <div class="meta m"><span style="color:var(--ink)">Build 05</span><span class="ln"></span><span>AI agent for service credits</span><span class="ln"></span><span><span class="rec"></span>Hackathon build</span></div>

 <section class="top">
  <div>
   <h1 style="font-size:clamp(70px,10.5vw,164px)">SLA Copilot</h1>
   <div class="kick">The site went down. Who do you owe money to? <span>And can you prove it landed?</span></div>
   <p class="lede">SLA Copilot turns a resolved production incident into verified service credits for the customers who were <b style="font-weight:500">actually</b> affected and contractually qualify. It joins incident, usage, plan and billing evidence, applies a policy gate, issues the credit in Stripe test mode, and independently checks the result.</p>
   <div class="acts"><a class="pillb solid" href="#run">Run the incident <span class="arr"></span></a><a class="pillb" href="#semantic">Why “200 OK” isn't enough</a></div>
  </div>
  <div class="facts rv">
   <div><span>Built at</span><span>A hackathon</span></div>
   <div><span>Role</span><span>Idea, pitch &amp; system design</span></div>
   <div><span>Systems</span><span>PagerDuty · PostHog · Stripe · Linear</span></div>
   <div><span>Policy</span><span>Deterministic, in YAML/JSON</span></div>
   <div><span>Safety</span><span>Stripe test mode · in-app approval</span></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 01 -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">01</span> · The problem</span><span>The question after the outage</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">Engineering knows what broke. Finance doesn't know who's owed.</h2><p class="rv d1">After an incident, teams answer the technical questions in minutes. The commercial one takes days, if anyone asks it at all. <b>SLA Copilot answers one narrow question with real money attached.</b></p></div>
  <div class="qs rv">
   <div><h5>EASY · ANSWERED IN MINUTES</h5><ul><li>What broke?<small>✓ KNOWN</small></li><li>When did it break?<small>✓ KNOWN</small></li><li>When did it recover?<small>✓ KNOWN</small></li></ul></div>
   <div><h5>HARD · USUALLY NOBODY ASKS</h5><div class="big">Which customers do we now owe something to?</div><p>And can we prove the credit reached the right customer, for the right amount, exactly once? SLA Copilot is proactive: it finds the obligation before an angry customer has to ask.</p></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 02 -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">02</span> · The key word</span><span>“Actually”</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">No single system knows the answer.</h2><p class="rv d1">Every tool holds one piece of the truth, and each piece on its own leads to the wrong decision. <b>The answer only exists once they're joined.</b></p></div>
  <div class="gaps">
   <div class="gp rv"><div class="a">An <b>outage</b> happened</div><div class="ne">≠</div><div class="c">which customers tried the broken feature</div></div>
   <div class="gp rv d1"><div class="a">A customer's <b>plan</b></div><div class="ne">≠</div><div class="c">whether this outage triggers their SLA credit</div></div>
   <div class="gp rv d2"><div class="a">A <b>successful Stripe call</b></div><div class="ne">≠</div><div class="c">the right customer got the right credit</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="join rv"><b>Operational events become customer decisions <em>only when joined with customer behavior.</em></b><span>THE CORE INSIGHT</span></div>
 </section>

 <!-- 03 SIM -->
 <section class="sec" id="run">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">03</span> · Run it</span><span>The hackathon demo, interactive</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">Run the incident yourself.</h2><p class="rv d1">This replays the demo scenario: a 47-minute SEV-1 outage of the exports service and three customers. Step through the state machine, approve the credit, then <b>replay it with a network timeout</b> to see why the verifier matters.</p></div>
  <div class="sim rv">
   <div class="sl2">
    <div class="inc"><div class="t"><span>PAGERDUTY · RESOLVED</span><span class="sev">SEV-1</span></div><h5>exports-api outage</h5>
     <div class="kv"><div>DURATION<b>47 min</b></div><div>SERVICE<b>exports-api</b></div></div></div>
    <div class="states" id="states"></div>
    <div class="ctl"><button class="p" id="next">Run SLA Copilot →</button><button class="x" id="chaos">Replay with a timeout</button><button id="reset">Reset</button></div>
   </div>
   <div class="sr"><div class="hdr"><b id="title">Ready</b><span id="sys">DEMO DATA · STRIPE TEST MODE</span></div><div id="pane" style="flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column"></div></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 04 -->
 <section class="sec" id="semantic">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">04</span> · Reliability</span><span>Transport vs. semantic success</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">A 200 OK can still be wrong.</h2><p class="rv d1">An agent can call every API successfully and still credit the wrong account, or credit the right one twice. So the success test is <b>the observed state of the systems</b>, not the agent's own trace.</p></div>
  <div class="ts rv">
   <div><h5>TRANSPORT SUCCESS</h5><div class="big">“Stripe returned 200.”</div><ul><li><i>?</i>Was it the right customer?</li><li><i>?</i>The authorized amount?</li><li><i>?</i>Under the right policy version?</li><li><i>?</i>Only once, even after a retry?</li></ul></div>
   <div><h5>SEMANTIC SUCCESS</h5><div class="big">“The state is correct.”</div><ul><li><i class="or">✓</i>Exact Stripe customer, from a stable mapping</li><li><i class="or">✓</i>Exact amount from the policy formula</li><li><i class="or">✓</i>Case and policy IDs in the metadata</li><li><i class="or">✓</i>Exactly one matching credit exists</li></ul></div>
  </div>
  <p class="claim rv">We don't close the case because the agent says it finished. <span>We close it when PagerDuty, PostHog, policy and Stripe agree.</span></p>
 </section>

 <!-- 05 -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">05</span> · Where the AI fits</span><span>Interpret, never decide money</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">The model explains. Code decides the money.</h2><p class="rv d1">A language model is good at reading messy incident text and writing clear summaries. It should never be the thing that <b>picks the account or computes the credit</b>.</p></div>
  <div class="mc2 rv">
   <div><h5>THE MODEL CAN</h5><div class="big">Interpret &amp; explain</div><ul><li>Read the incident and name the affected feature</li><li>Summarize the evidence for a reviewer</li><li>Write a short audit explanation</li><li>Flag ambiguity and ask a human</li></ul></div>
   <div><h5>DETERMINISTIC CODE MUST</h5><div class="big">Decide &amp; act</div><ul><li>Map each account to its exact Stripe customer</li><li>Evaluate policy rules and compute the credit</li><li>Enforce approval limits and idempotency</li><li>Make the Stripe write, then verify it</li></ul></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 06 -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">06</span> · The systems</span><span>Each one holds a piece</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">Five tools, one decision.</h2><p class="rv d1">The core loop runs on three systems. Linear and Slack were <b>scoped as bonuses</b>, so the demo couldn't break on an integration that wasn't essential.</p></div>
  <div class="sys">
   <div class="sy rv"><div class="h"><span>01</span><span class="st b">CORE</span></div><h5>PagerDuty</h5><p>Incident facts: service, severity, start, end and status.</p></div>
   <div class="sy rv d1"><div class="h"><span>02</span><span class="st b">CORE</span></div><h5>PostHog</h5><p>Which accounts actually hit the broken feature during the window.</p></div>
   <div class="sy rv d2"><div class="h"><span>03</span><span class="st b">CORE</span></div><h5>Stripe</h5><p>Customer and plan lookup, then the credit, in test mode.</p></div>
   <div class="sy rv"><div class="h"><span>04</span><span class="st">BONUS</span></div><h5>Linear</h5><p>One audit issue per incident with every decision linked.</p></div>
   <div class="sy rv d1"><div class="h"><span>05</span><span class="st f">DEFERRED</span></div><h5>Slack</h5><p>Approval for large credits. In-app approval used instead.</p></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 07 -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">07</span> · Picking the idea</span><span>Narrow beats generic</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">Why this idea, and not the obvious ones.</h2><p class="rv d1">I wanted a problem the hackathon's host companies actually face, with one clear, consequential decision that could be <b>explained and demoed in two minutes</b>.</p></div>
  <div class="alt">
   <div class="ar rv"><h5>Refund / support agent</h5><span>Too easy to shrink into an email parser that calls Stripe.</span><span class="s">PASSED</span></div>
   <div class="ar rv"><h5>Incident commander</h5><span>Useful but broad. The demo turns into a pile of dashboards.</span><span class="s">PASSED</span></div>
   <div class="ar rv"><h5>Coding agent</h5><span>Another “read ticket, change code” demo.</span><span class="s">PASSED</span></div>
   <div class="ar rv"><h5>Cross-app reconciler</h5><span>Interesting, but abstract to explain fast.</span><span class="s">PASSED</span></div>
   <div class="ar win rv"><h5>SLA Copilot</h5><span>A concrete domain, a visible financial result and an obvious definition of success.</span><span class="s">CHOSEN</span></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 08 -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">08</span> · Honest limits</span><span>What a real version needs</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">A decision assistant, not an autopilot for payouts.</h2><p class="rv d1">The prototype proves a <b>safe execution loop</b>. A production version would need more, and I'd rather say so than oversell it.</p></div>
  <div class="lim">
   <div class="rv"><small>01 · IDENTITY</small><h5>Account mapping</h5><p>Real companies have messy IDs across tools. Production needs a trusted identity service, not string matching.</p></div>
   <div class="rv d1"><small>02 · CONTRACTS</small><h5>SLAs vary</h5><p>Real contracts have caps, exclusions and maintenance clauses. The demo policy is illustrative.</p></div>
   <div class="rv d2"><small>03 · EVIDENCE</small><h5>Attempt ≠ impact</h5><p>A failed event proves an attempt, not every business impact. Raw event IDs stay in the audit.</p></div>
   <div class="rv"><small>04 · SAFETY</small><h5>Capped autonomy</h5><p>Scoped credentials, amount caps, approval thresholds and a reconciliation job.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="chips rv"><span>Python</span><span>PagerDuty MCP / API</span><span>PostHog</span><span>Stripe test mode</span><span>Linear</span><span>YAML policy engine</span><span>State machines</span><span>Idempotency</span></div>
 </section>

 <a class="next rv" href="/projects/orion"><div><div class="m">Next project · Back to the top</div><h2>ORION</h2><p>An AI analyst that refuses to guess.</p></div><span class="bigarr"><span class="arr"></span></span></a>
</main>
